A load test of the session lifetime. NOT part of `make check`: it needs a live REALITY server, and
the checks are required to run offline.

    docker start xhttp-test
    make BUILD=build-asan \
      CFLAGS="-std=c11 -O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -Wall \
              -Isrc -Ibuild-asan/gen -D_GNU_SOURCE -DLC_HYSTERIA=1 -MMD -MP" \
      LDFLAGS="-fsanitize=address,undefined" build-asan/lc-tunnel
    python3 test/storm/storm.py

What is checked, and why in this shape. The real use-after-free in this loop happened when both
descriptors of one session landed in the same epoll batch — so the storm opens connections in
batches and tears them down mixed: half of them politely, half through SO_LINGER=0, which is a RST
with unread data still queued. The sanitiser IS the assertion here: touching a freed session, a
double free and a write past a buffer are things it names by itself.

Two settings give different coverage, and both are needed. A short pause before the teardown hits
the early lifetime (connecting and the handshake); a pause of about a third of a second lets the
handshake finish, and then what is torn down are WORKING sessions. The first run without the second
exercises the wrong path, and that is measured rather than assumed: tearing down immediately, 5
sessions out of 32448 ever reached ST_OPEN.

Runs on record: 32448 connections down the early path and 680 down the working one (480 of them in
ST_OPEN) — zero ASan/UBSan reports, zero leaks, the process alive at the end.
